[[File:Hairbrush flat.JPG|250px|thumb|Matu suka]]
[[File:A Toothbrush.JPG|250px|thumb|Zobu suka]]

===Etymology 1===
There are competing theories on the origin of this word. The first (and more probable) one assumes that it comes from {{uder|lv|ine-pro|*suH-||pig}} > derived form ''*suk-'' “pig” (masc., fem.), “coarse hair, bristle” (neut.). Originally {{m|lv|suka}} was the neuter plural form, but it became feminine singular after the general loss of the neuter gender, with semantic change “bristles” > “brush” (compare {{cog|de|Bürste||brush}}, {{m|de|Borste||bristle}}). Borrowings by Finnic languages from Baltic languages support this evolution (e.g., {{cog|fi|suka||pig bristle (''archaic''), currycomb}}). The second theory considers {{m|lv|suka|}} cognate to {{cog|lt|šùkos||brush}}, {{cog|ru|щётка||brush}}, {{cog|sa|शूक||needle}}, all from Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*ḱū-||pointed}}. A third theory attributes these words to Proto-Indo-European {{m|ine-pro|*kes-}}, {{m|ine-pro|*ks-||to cut, to carve}}.<ref>{{R:lv:LEV}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{lv-noun|f|4th}}

# [[brush]] {{gloss|[[instrument]] made with [[flexible]] [[bristle]]s attached to a [[handle]], used especially for [[groom]]ing [[hair]]}}
#: {{ux|lv|drēbju, apavu '''suka'''|clothes, shoe '''brush'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|matu '''suka'''|hair'''brush'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|zobu '''suka'''|tooth'''brush'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|zirgu '''suka'''|horse '''brush''', currycomb|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|saru, tērauda '''suka'''|bristle, steel '''brush'''|inline=1}}
#: {{ux|lv|tīrīt drēbes ar '''suku'''|to clean (one's) clothes with a '''brush'''|inline=1}}

